* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    ok = !tier_allows(user, account)
*
*    status()  0  allowed
*              1  the ACCOUNT has no usable tier, or is not in the register
*              2  the USER has no usable tier, or is not in the register
*              3  refused: the user's tier is below the account's
*              4  refused: SDSYS is never granted to anybody
*
*    OWNER'S RULE, 29 Aug 2026 (to the port): "standard accounts can not be
*    given access to programmer accounts, programmer accounts can be given
*    access to standard accounts.  Only windows administrators can enter SDSYS,
*    rights to SDSYS can not be granted to programmers or standard accounts."
*    Here the first clause reads the same and the second is "only an
*    administrator enters SDSYS", which CPROC's int.logto already enforces.
*
*    SO A GRANT MAY GO DOWN OR SIDEWAYS, NEVER UP.  STANDARD 1, PROGRAMMER 2,
*    ADMINISTRATOR 3, and entry is permitted when the person's rank is at least
*    the account's.  Equal ranks pass: two people sharing an account is the
*    ordinary case and not what the rule is aimed at.
*
*    ONE PLACE THE DECISION IS MADE, which is why this is a catalogued function
*    and not a test copied into three programs.  Three callers: CPROC's LOGTO,
*    GRANTA, and MODIFY.ACCOUNT's ADD arm.
*
*    THE REAL GATE IS CPROC'S AND THE OTHER TWO ARE COURTESY.  The grant IS
*    Linux group membership, so an administrator can add somebody to sdu_<name>
*    with usermod and never come near SD.  A check only at grant time would
*    also go stale by itself: MODIFY.ACCOUNT can promote an account after a
*    legal grant, and nothing revisits the group afterwards.  Refusing at LOGTO
*    is what actually holds.
*
*    SUSPENDED IS RESOLVED THROUGH FIELD 6, NOT TREATED AS A TIER OF ITS OWN.
*    ACC$PRIOR.TIER exists so the displaced tier is not lost, so a suspended
*    person keeps their rank here and a grant made while they are suspended is
*    still the right one when the suspension lifts.  Their access is denied by
*    LOGIN and by LOGTO, which is where a suspension belongs; making it a rank
*    as well would refuse grants for a second reason and leave the
*    administrator re-granting after every unsuspend.  This is deliberately the
*    OPPOSITE of CPROC's grant.administrator, which asks whether somebody is an
*    administrator RIGHT NOW and a suspended one is not.
*
*    AN UNKNOWN OR EMPTY TIER IS REFUSED, NOT DEFAULTED, and the direction is
*    deliberate.  It is the rule GRANTA applies to an empty ACC$GROUP - refused
*    rather than guessed - and the OPPOSITE of NEWVOC's tier lists, where a
*    missing record means the FULL VOC.  Do not carry that convention across.
*
*    SDSYS IS REFUSED BY NAME AS WELL AS BY TIER.  Its shipped ACCOUNTS record
*    carries three fields - path, description, group - and no ACC$TIER, so the
*    rank test below already refuses it; and its ACC$GROUP is "sdsys", so a
*    membership test could never pass either.  Both are accidents of the seed
*    record rather than statements of the rule, and both would evaporate if
*    somebody gave SDSYS a tier.  The rule is absolute, so it is written down
*    as an absolute.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
$flags trusted

function tier_allows(user, account)
$catalog !tier_allows

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   set.status 0

   acct = upcase(trim(account, ' ', 'B'))
   who  = upcase(trim(user, ' ', 'B'))

*  SDSYS IS NEVER GRANTED.  Owner's rule; see above for why this is stated
*  rather than left to the two accidents that also stop it.
   if acct = 'SDSYS' then
      set.status 4
      return @false
   end

   if acct = '' or who = '' then
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'ACCOUNTS' to tg.acc.f else
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   read tg.tgt.rec from tg.acc.f, acct else
      close tg.acc.f
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   read tg.own.rec from tg.acc.f, who else
      close tg.acc.f
      set.status 2
      return @false
   end

   close tg.acc.f

*  THE ORDER IS THE RANK.  Position in this list is the number compared below,
*  so adding a tier means putting it in the right place rather than editing a
*  case statement somewhere else as well.
   tg.tiers = 'STANDARD' : @fm : 'PROGRAMMER' : @fm : 'ADMINISTRATOR'

   tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.tgt.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   if tg.t = 'SUSPENDED' then
      tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.tgt.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   end
   locate tg.t in tg.tiers<1> setting tg.tgt.rank else tg.tgt.rank = 0

   tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.own.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   if tg.t = 'SUSPENDED' then
      tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.own.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   end
   locate tg.t in tg.tiers<1> setting tg.own.rank else tg.own.rank = 0

   if tg.tgt.rank = 0 then
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   if tg.own.rank = 0 then
      set.status 2
      return @false
   end

   if tg.own.rank < tg.tgt.rank then
      set.status 3
      return @false
   end

   set.status 0
   return @true

end

* END-CODE
